---
/*
 * /about — who builds Empirical BBQ and why.
 *
 * This page exists to attach a real, named, accountable person to the numbers
 * the calculators publish. It carries the site's `Person` schema (from
 * src/utils/author.js, shared with the blog bylines and /methodology) so the
 * visible bio and the structured data always agree.
 *
 * Editorial rule for this page: claim only what's true. The author is a
 * software/data professional who cooks — not a chef, food scientist, or
 * competition pitmaster — and the page says so plainly. The site's credibility
 * rests on citing published sources and showing the math on /methodology, so
 * overstating credentials here would undercut the very thing that makes the
 * tools trustworthy.
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_ROLE, AUTHOR_SCHEMA, AUTHOR_URL } from '../utils/author.js';
import { PROTEINS } from '../utils/proteinRegistry.js';
import { TOOL_COUNT } from '../utils/toolMatrix.js';

const proteinCount = Object.keys(PROTEINS).length;

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'AboutPage',
    name: 'About Empirical BBQ',
    description:
      'Who builds Empirical BBQ, why the calculators exist, how the models are sourced and tested, and how the site is funded.',
    url: AUTHOR_URL,
    mainEntity: AUTHOR_SCHEMA,
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    ...AUTHOR_SCHEMA,
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="About | Who Builds Empirical BBQ"
  description="Empirical BBQ is built by Mike Merrill — a software and data professional who got tired of guessing at cook times and serving sizes. Who's behind the models, how they're sourced and tested, and how the site is funded."
  ogUrl={AUTHOR_URL}
  active="about"
  bodyClass="page-about min-h-screen flex flex-col bg-base-850 text-base-300 font-display antialiased selection:bg-flame-500/30"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="relative z-10 flex-1 w-full">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-12 sm:py-16">
      <header class="mb-10">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-flame-500/10 ring-1 ring-flame-500/40 px-3.5 py-1.5 mb-5">
          <span class="text-sm">👋</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide text-flame-300 uppercase">About</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white leading-[1.08]">
          Who builds Empirical BBQ
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base sm:text-lg text-base-400 leading-relaxed">
          Empirical BBQ is built and maintained by one person: <strong class="text-base-300">{AUTHOR_NAME}</strong>.
          Not an agency, not a content farm, and not a chef with a cookbook to sell — just someone who
          kept missing the mark on his own cooks and decided to go find the actual numbers.
        </p>
      </header>

      <!-- ============ THE PERSON ============ -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-flame-400 mb-4">The person behind it</h2>
        <div class="rounded-2xl bg-base-800/60 ring-1 ring-base-700 p-5 sm:p-6 space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-[15px] text-base-400 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I'm {AUTHOR_NAME}, the {AUTHOR_ROLE.replace(/^Creator of /, 'creator of ')}. I hold a
            <strong class="text-base-300">B.S. in Computer Science</strong> and have spent about
            <strong class="text-base-300">ten years in a senior technical role in the public sector</strong>
            in Las&nbsp;Vegas, Nevada. Most of that career has come down to the same task over and over:
            take a system that isn't working, find the data nobody has bothered to look at, and use it to
            fix the thing for real instead of guessing at it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Outside of work I'm a husband and a father of three, which is its own lesson in cooking for a
            deadline. I've been <strong class="text-base-300">seriously cooking, grilling, and smoking for
            about two years</strong> — long enough to have made most of the classic mistakes, and recently
            enough that I still remember exactly how it feels to have guests arriving at six with a brisket
            that has been sitting at 160°F for three hours.
          </p>
          <p>
            I want to be straightforward about that, because it matters for how you should read this site:
            <strong class="text-base-300">I'm not a chef, a food scientist, or a competition pitmaster.</strong>
            What I am is someone who is good at finding, modeling, and stress-testing data — and who applied
            that to a hobby where an unusual amount of the advice turns out to be folklore repeated with
            confidence.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ WHY ============ -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-flame-400 mb-4">Why this site exists</h2>
        <div class="rounded-2xl bg-base-800/60 ring-1 ring-base-700 p-5 sm:p-6 space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-[15px] text-base-400 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            I've always been more interested in <em class="text-base-300">why</em> something works than in
            being told that it does. When I started cooking for people, I kept missing on the three things
            that actually determine whether a cook succeeds: <strong class="text-base-300">timing</strong>
            (the meat wasn't ready when people were), <strong class="text-base-300">quantity</strong> (I bought
            the wrong amount and either ran short or ate leftovers for a week), and
            <strong class="text-base-300">quality</strong> (the result was inconsistent in ways I couldn't explain).
          </p>
          <p>
            Every time I went looking for an answer, I got the same thing back: a rule of thumb with no
            reasoning attached. "About an hour and a quarter per pound." "Half a pound per person." "Wrap it
            at 165." Some of that folklore is roughly right. Some of it is off by hours. None of it tells you
            <em class="text-base-300">which</em> — or what changes when your brisket is 18&nbsp;lb instead of 12,
            when it's 40°F and windy, or when you're running a kamado instead of an offset.
          </p>
          <p>
            So I went into the raw data and the food science behind it: USDA yield figures, published
            heat-transfer and evaporative-cooling work, and field cook-time consensus from people who cook far
            more than I do. Then I did the thing I do professionally — turned it into models, wrote down every
            constant and where it came from, and locked the outputs with regression tests so the numbers can't
            quietly drift. That's what these {TOOL_COUNT} calculators are, across {proteinCount} proteins.
          </p>
          <p>
            The goal isn't to replace your judgment or your thermometer. It's to replace
            <em class="text-base-300">guessing</em> with a stated estimate you can see the reasoning behind —
            so when you plan a cook, you know what the model assumed and where it might be wrong.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ HOW YOU CAN CHECK ME ============ -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-flame-400 mb-4">How to check my work</h2>
        <div class="rounded-2xl bg-base-800/60 ring-1 ring-base-700 p-5 sm:p-6 space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-[15px] text-base-400 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Because I'm not asking you to trust my credentials, the site is built so you don't have to.
            Everything is inspectable:
          </p>
          <ul class="space-y-3 pl-1">
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-flame-400 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-base-300">Every equation and constant is published.</strong> The
                <a href="/methodology" class="text-flame-400 hover:text-flame-300 underline underline-offset-2">methodology page</a>
                lists the models, the per-protein calibration constants, and their sources. Those tables are
                generated from the same registry the calculators run on, so the published numbers cannot drift
                from the computed ones.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-flame-400 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-base-300">Estimates are labeled as estimates.</strong> Where a constant is a
                calibration guess rather than a sourced figure, it's marked as one. Where the honest answer is a
                range, the tools show a range instead of inventing precision.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-flame-400 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-base-300">Model changes are versioned and logged.</strong> Each protein model
                carries a version number shown in the results panel, and material recalibrations are written up in
                the changelog on the methodology page — including the time I found the model was running roughly
                45% fast and had to refit it.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex gap-3">
              <span class="text-flame-400 shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-base-300">Every calculation runs client-side.</strong> There are no
                accounts, and by default your cook details aren't sent anywhere. The Stall Predictor's
                cook log is the one opt-in exception — off unless you turn it on, and anonymous even
                then: no name, email, or precise location, ever. See the
                <a href="/privacy" class="text-flame-400 hover:text-flame-300 underline underline-offset-2">privacy policy</a>
                for exactly what that sends.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="text-[13px] text-base-500 pt-1">
            If you find a number that doesn't match your results, I genuinely want to hear it — that's the
            fastest way these models get better. <a href="/contact" class="text-flame-400 hover:text-flame-300 underline underline-offset-2">Tell me what happened</a>,
            including your cut, weight, pit, and what actually took how long.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ FUNDING / TRANSPARENCY ============ -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-flame-400 mb-4">How the site is funded</h2>
        <div class="rounded-2xl bg-base-800/60 ring-1 ring-base-700 p-5 sm:p-6 space-y-4 text-sm sm:text-[15px] text-base-400 leading-relaxed">
          <p>
            Every tool here is free and always will be. The site is supported by
            <strong class="text-base-300">display advertising</strong> and by
            <strong class="text-base-300">affiliate links</strong> — as an Amazon Associate I earn from
            qualifying purchases, and gear recommendations are marked as such where they appear.
          </p>
          <p>
            The line I hold: <strong class="text-base-300">advertising never touches the models.</strong> No
            calculator output is tuned to sell anything, affiliate links never appear inside results copy,
            and a product being linked has no bearing on what the math says. If a recommendation and the
            model ever disagree, the model wins and the recommendation goes.
          </p>
          <p class="text-[13px] text-base-500">
            See the <a href="/privacy" class="text-flame-400 hover:text-flame-300 underline underline-offset-2">privacy policy</a>
            for what's collected (anonymous analytics, with a consent banner) and what isn't.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ THE DISCLAIMER THAT MATTERS ============ -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <div class="rounded-2xl bg-flame-500/10 ring-1 ring-flame-500/30 p-5 sm:p-6">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">One thing these tools will never do</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-base-300 leading-relaxed">
            They will not tell you your food is done. Every output on this site is a
            <strong class="text-white">planning estimate</strong> — useful for deciding when to light the fire
            and how much to buy, and not a substitute for a thermometer. Cuts, pits, and weather vary more than
            any model can capture. Build in a buffer, and always probe to temperature.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <nav class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm font-medium">
        <a href="/" class="text-flame-400 hover:text-flame-300 transition-colors">← Home</a>
        <a href="/methodology" class="text-base-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Methodology</a>
        <a href="/brisket-calculator" class="text-base-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Calculators</a>
        <a href="/blog" class="text-base-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Blog</a>
        <a href="/contact" class="text-base-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Contact</a>
        <a href="/privacy" class="text-base-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Privacy</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style is:global>
  body.page-about::before {
    content: '';
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
    background:
      radial-gradient(1200px 600px at 82% -12%, rgba(249, 115, 22, 0.14), transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(950px 520px at -8% 108%, rgba(245, 158, 11, 0.09), transparent 55%);
  }
</style>
